   "title": "Quadratic Formula & Completing the Square",
   "content": "<p>The <strong>quadratic formula</strong> solves \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\): \\[x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\]</p><p>The <strong>discriminant</strong> \\(b^2 - 4ac\\) tells you about the roots: positive = 2 real roots, zero = 1 repeated root, negative = no real roots.</p><p><strong>Completing the square</strong> rewrites \\(x^2 + bx + c\\) as \\((x + \\frac{b}{2})^2 - (\\frac{b}{2})^2 + c\\). This gives the turning point directly: \\((x + p)^2 + q\\) has turning point \\((-p, q)\\).</p><p>For \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) where \\(a \\neq 1\\): factor out \\(a\\) first, then complete the square inside.</p>",
   "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(2x^2 + 3x - 5 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> \\(a=2, b=3, c=-5\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(b^2-4ac = 9+40 = 49\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> \\(x = \\frac{-3 \\pm 7}{4}\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(x = 1\\) or \\(x = -\\frac{5}{2}\\)</p>"
  },
